WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.000
Can we be offline without
anyone breaking in?

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:06.220
A DDoS attack can do it, in four ways.

00:00:07.583 --> 00:00:09.273
First, the pipe fills up.

00:00:09.498 --> 00:00:13.548
Hundreds of thousands of hijacked
devices send traffic at once.

00:00:13.873 --> 00:00:18.143
Our connection has a fixed size,
so real customers cannot get through.

00:00:18.393 --> 00:00:22.823
Only capacity in front of us,
bigger than any attacker, absorbs it.

00:00:23.783 --> 00:00:25.533
Second, expensive requests.

00:00:25.993 --> 00:00:30.003
Bots ask for product searches,
logins and password resets.

00:00:30.343 --> 00:00:33.673
The dashboards say the site is up,
but nobody can use it.

00:00:33.913 --> 00:00:37.383
Capping the expensive actions
helps more than adding servers.

00:00:38.583 --> 00:00:40.913
Third, one address book for everything.

00:00:41.228 --> 00:00:43.058
Customers find us through DNS.

00:00:43.598 --> 00:00:47.968
If that provider fails, our website,
email and app vanish at once.

00:00:48.308 --> 00:00:50.318
It is rarely on the risk register.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:52.580
Fourth, the timing.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.870
Attackers pick our busiest day.

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:59.020
A short demonstration attack
is followed by a demand to pay.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.450
Paying proves that we pay,
and invites the next demand.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:04.760
What good looks like.

00:01:05.045 --> 00:01:08.955
A CDN with scrubbing sits in
front of everything public.

00:01:09.225 --> 00:01:12.595
Two independent DNS
providers answer for us.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.790
Rate limiting and bot management
protect the expensive pages.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:21.190
A failover page and a tested
runbook keep customers informed.

00:01:21.545 --> 00:01:26.835
NIS2 names business continuity and crisis
management as risk-management measures.

00:01:28.697 --> 00:01:29.907
A suggested first step.

00:01:30.192 --> 00:01:33.502
Ask what one hour offline
costs on our busiest day.

00:01:33.712 --> 00:01:38.122
Then ask who, by name, may switch the
mitigation on at three in the morning.

00:01:38.412 --> 00:01:41.762
If either answer takes longer
than a minute, that is the gap.

00:01:42.057 --> 00:01:44.987
Consider deciding in
advance that we do not pay.

00:01:46.517 --> 00:01:48.887
Availability is revenue per hour.

00:01:49.147 --> 00:01:53.177
Put capacity in front of us,
and decide before the demand arrives.

